## Validate before submit

In ARC stream interfaces, a correct inference can still waste a submission if the emitted grid is malformed. Build the output separately from the reply, then check the exact final representation:

- output row count equals input height;
- every row contains exactly input width cells;
- all values are allowed colors (typically integers 0–9);
- unchanged cells remain unchanged unless the inferred rule requires otherwise;
- the cells changed in the JSON match the coordinate-level transformation map.

If using a script or helper to construct the grid, serialize and inspect the exact serialized grid that will be submitted, not a manually retyped approximation. Return only the protocol-required action object when the interface requires that format.

## Make demonstrations discriminate

After each demonstration, enumerate changed coordinates and compare changed and unchanged components. Record the ambiguity that remains (for example, whether selection is by shape, orientation, proximity, or alignment). Request another demo only when its possible outcomes would distinguish the leading hypotheses. Avoid repeated open-ended speculation; translate alternatives into explicit predictions before spending another request. Since demo requests and wrong submissions both cost points, prefer a supported answer over excessive querying, but do not submit an output whose dimensions have not been checked.

## Session lesson

One ARC stream submission was rejected as an invalid grid because a row was omitted from an otherwise plausible output. Treat structural validity as an independent pre-submit check, separate from reasoning correctness.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-medium-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u026/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzles/continual-arc-grid-reasoning/references/submission-validation-and-demo-economics.md"}

